{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Each man his shelach</b> — his sword. He means that they do not remove their garments, nor do they set down their weapons, for when they go to the feast at night their weapons are in their hands. And some say: it is from the Aramaic translation, <b>ופשיט וישלח</b> ('and he removed [his garment] and sent [his hand] = drew his weapon')." "<b>And the meaning of שלחו</b> — its removal. And likewise the interpretation: we do not remove our garments except a man who needs washing and purification — he alone would remove his garments, and this is <b>shilchu ha-mayim</b> ('their sending forth the water'). And this is very correct." "<b>Mishmeret, melachah</b> — missing the lamed of melachah, and the correct reading is <b>la-mishmeret la-melachah</b>."], :book :nehemiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Nehemiah -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/nechemya"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:15:06.020929708Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}